title: Human Body Systems
subject: Biology
level: GCSE
emoji: 🫀
description: Digestion, circulation, breathing, nervous system and hormones.
---
Which enzyme breaks down starch, and where is it made? :: Amylase, made in the salivary glands and pancreas.
Which enzyme breaks down proteins? :: Protease (e.g. pepsin in the stomach).
Which enzyme breaks down fats? :: Lipase, producing fatty acids and glycerol.
What does bile do? :: Emulsifies fat to increase its surface area and neutralises stomach acid.
Where is bile made and stored? :: Made in the liver, stored in the gall bladder.
Where does most absorption of food happen? :: The small intestine (villi give a large surface area).
What are the four chambers of the heart? :: Right atrium, right ventricle, left atrium, left ventricle.
Which blood vessel carries blood from the heart to the lungs? :: Pulmonary artery.
Which blood vessel carries oxygenated blood from the lungs to the heart? :: Pulmonary vein.
Why is the left ventricle wall thicker than the right? :: It pumps blood around the whole body at higher pressure.
What does the aorta do? :: Carries oxygenated blood from the left ventricle to the body.
Name the three types of blood vessel. :: Arteries, veins and capillaries.
What do red blood cells do? :: Carry oxygen using haemoglobin; they have no nucleus and a biconcave shape.
What do white blood cells do? :: Defend against pathogens by phagocytosis and by producing antibodies and antitoxins.
What are platelets for? :: Help blood to clot.
What is the site of gas exchange in the lungs? :: The alveoli.
What is the path of a nerve impulse in a reflex arc? :: Receptor → sensory neurone → relay neurone → motor neurone → effector.
What gland is the 'master gland'? :: The pituitary gland.
Which hormone lowers blood glucose? :: Insulin (from the pancreas).
Which hormone raises blood glucose? :: Glucagon (from the pancreas).
What is homeostasis? :: Maintaining a constant internal environment (temperature, water, glucose).
